<% content_for(:title, "ForgeCI — CircleCI-compatible CI/CD you run yourself") %>

<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow"><b>Open source</b> · MIT licensed · self-hosted</span>
      <h1>CircleCI configs.<br><span class="grad">Your runners.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        ForgeCI runs your existing <code>.circleci/config.yml</code> on infrastructure you control.
        Push to GitHub and Docker runners pick up the work, stream logs live and report back to your pull requests.
      </p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="#quickstart"><%= icon "terminal" %> Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-lg" href="https://github.com/azmi2409/forge-ci"><%= icon "github" %> View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <div class="proof">
        <span><%= icon "check" %> CircleCI 2.1 configs</span>
        <span><%= icon "check" %> Outbound-only runners</span>
        <span><%= icon "check" %> No per-minute bill</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal" aria-label="Example config">
      <div class="terminal-bar"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>.circleci/config.yml</div>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Your existing config. No rewrite.</span>
<span class="k">version</span>: <span class="s">2.1</span>

<span class="k">jobs</span>:
  <span class="k">test</span>:
    <span class="k">docker</span>:
      - <span class="k">image</span>: <span class="s">cimg/ruby:3.3</span>
    <span class="k">resource_class</span>: <span class="s">spot</span>
    <span class="k">parallelism</span>: <span class="s">4</span>
    <span class="k">steps</span>:
      - <span class="k">checkout</span>
      - <span class="k">run</span>: <span class="s">bundle install</span>
      - <span class="k">run</span>: |
          <span class="s">circleci tests glob "test/**/*_test.rb" |</span>
            <span class="s">circleci tests run --split-by=filesize \</span>
              <span class="s">--command="xargs bin/rails test"</span>

<span class="k">workflows</span>:
  <span class="k">ci</span>:
    <span class="k">jobs</span>: [<span class="s">test</span>]</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="features">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">Features</div>
    <h2 class="h2">Everything you use in CircleCI, on your own hardware</h2>
    <p class="sub">A Rails control plane, a pure-Ruby config compiler and zero-dependency Docker runners. Boring technology you can operate.</p>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card feature">
        <div class="fi"><%= icon "layers" %></div>
        <h3>CircleCI 2.1 compatible</h3>
        <p>Executors, commands, parameters, workflows, <code>requires</code>, branch filters and <code>matrix</code>. The compiler mirrors CircleCI's own compile semantics.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <div class="fi"><%= icon "cpu" %></div>
        <h3>Runners anywhere</h3>
        <p>Runners poll for work over outbound HTTPS, so they run behind NAT. Route jobs by <code>resource_class</code>, e.g. checks on Spot, deploys on on-demand.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <div class="fi"><%= icon "activity" %></div>
        <h3>Parallel test splitting</h3>
        <p><code>parallelism: N</code> plus <code>circleci tests glob | split | run</code>. Split your suite by name or file size across as many runners as you have.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <div class="fi"><%= icon "terminal" %></div>
        <h3>Live logs</h3>
        <p>Every step's output streams to the dashboard as it happens, with secrets masked before they leave the runner.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <div class="fi"><%= icon "folder" %></div>
        <h3>Caches, workspaces, artifacts</h3>
        <p><code>save_cache</code>/<code>restore_cache</code> with checksum keys, workspaces between jobs and downloadable artifacts, stored in S3.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <div class="fi"><%= icon "github" %></div>
        <h3>GitHub native</h3>
        <p>A GitHub App starts pipelines on push and pull request, clones private repos with short-lived tokens and posts commit statuses back.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="how-it-works">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">How it works</div>
    <h2 class="h2">From <code>git push</code> to a green check</h2>
    <p class="sub">No inbound ports on your runners and no proprietary control plane.</p>
    <div class="steps3">
      <div class="card step">
        <h3>Push</h3>
        <p>The GitHub App receives the webhook and fetches <code>.circleci/config.yml</code> at that commit. Or start a run from any CI with <code>forge-ci trigger</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card step">
        <h3>Compile</h3>
        <p>The config is validated and compiled into workflows and jobs, with a dependency graph. Bad configs fail fast with a clear error on the commit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card step">
        <h3>Run</h3>
        <p>Runners claim queued jobs, run each step in Docker and stream logs back. Jobs that depend on others start as soon as those pass.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="compatibility">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">Compatibility</div>
    <h2 class="h2">Honest about what's supported</h2>
    <p class="sub">The CircleCI 2.1 features most pipelines use work today. The rest fail with a clear error instead of misbehaving.</p>
    <div class="compat">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-head"><h3>Supported</h3></div>
        <ul>
          <li class="yes"><%= icon "check" %><span>Docker executors, service containers, <code>executors:</code>, <code>commands:</code>, parameters</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><%= icon "check" %><span>Workflows, <code>requires</code>, <code>matrix</code>, branch filters, contexts</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><%= icon "check" %><span>Caches, workspaces, artifacts, <code>when:</code> conditions, timeouts</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><%= icon "check" %><span><code>parallelism</code> and <code>circleci tests</code> splitting</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><%= icon "check" %><span>Bash steps, <code>$BASH_ENV</code>, <code>CIRCLE_*</code> variables</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-head"><h3>Not yet</h3></div>
        <ul>
          <li class="no"><%= icon "x" %><span>Orbs (no registry yet)</span></li>
          <li class="no"><%= icon "x" %><span><code>machine</code>, <code>macos</code> and <code>windows</code> executors</span></li>
          <li class="no"><%= icon "x" %><span>Approval jobs, dynamic config, scheduled pipelines</span></li>
          <li class="no"><%= icon "x" %><span>Timing-based splitting, <code>setup_remote_docker</code></span></li>
          <li class="no"><%= icon "x" %><span>Docker layer caching, SSH re-runs</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="architecture">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">Architecture</div>
    <h2 class="h2">One control plane, as many runners as you need</h2>
    <p class="sub">Postgres for state, S3 for logs and artifacts. Runners hold nothing, so you scale by adding hosts.</p>
    <div class="card arch">
<pre>  <span class="hl">GitHub</span> ── push / PR webhook ──▶ ┌─────────────────────┐ ◀── claim · heartbeat · logs ── <span class="hl">Runner</span> (Docker)
          ◀── commit statuses ─── │   <span class="hl">Control plane</span>     │ ◀── claim · heartbeat · logs ── <span class="hl">Runner</span> (Docker)
                                  │   Rails 8 · web UI  │ ◀── claim · heartbeat · logs ── <span class="hl">Runner</span> (Docker)
                                  └──────┬───────┬──────┘
                                         │       │
                                   <span class="hl">Postgres</span>     <span class="hl">S3</span>
                                 jobs, state   logs, caches, artifacts</pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="quickstart">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">Quickstart</div>
    <h2 class="h2">Up in five minutes</h2>
    <p class="sub">Postgres, the control plane, the worker and a runner from one compose file.</p>
    <div class="terminal">
      <div class="terminal-bar"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>terminal</div>
<pre><code><span class="p">$</span> git clone https://github.com/azmi2409/forge-ci && cd forge-ci
<span class="p">$</span> cp .env.example .env          <span class="c"># S3 bucket + credentials, secrets</span>
<span class="p">$</span> openssl rand -hex 32          <span class="c"># paste into .env as RUNNER_TOKEN</span>
<span class="p">$</span> docker compose up --build -d
<span class="p">$</span> docker compose up -d runner
<span class="c">  → open http://localhost:3000 and sign in</span></code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Create the S3 bucket first (<code>aws s3 mb s3://&lt;bucket&gt;</code>). For production (HTTPS, the GitHub App, runners on
      their own hosts) follow <a href="https://github.com/azmi2409/forge-ci/blob/main/DEPLOY.md">DEPLOY.md</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block final">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Stop paying per minute for your own tests</h2>
    <p class="sub">Point ForgeCI at the config you already have and run it on hardware you already own.</p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="#quickstart">Get started</a>
      <a class="btn btn-lg" href="/projects">Open the dashboard</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
